<%# One line in the river (D-feed-reader, D-reader-density). Google Reader's
    row, which nothing since has improved on — source, headline, the first
    few words, the age — with two changes the phone forced.

    The first is that it no longer wraps. The old row was `flex-wrap` below
    `sm` with a full-width verb line, which took its own line and then
    wrapped again under source + age + chip + three verbs: ~96px to say
    less than the 34px desktop row said. This one is a fixed height at every
    width, and the face carries the identity that a 45%-truncated feed title
    was carrying badly.

    The second is that it opens *here*. A plain click expands the row in
    place — the blurb, and the verbs at a size a thumb can hit — rather than
    throwing the reader out to a new tab, which is what made a four-hundred
    item river something you could only ever triage. A modified or middle
    click still goes straight to the source, because that is somebody
    stashing tabs and it is not our business to redirect them.

    Text and a link out, never the publisher's HTML (it's stripped at parse
    time), because the article belongs to the site that wrote it.

    Locals: item; optional filter — the list this row is being rendered
    under, so a row marked read while you are looking at the *unread* list
    knows to leave it; optional expanded — whether it comes back open, which
    is how the row survives the re-render its own mark-read triggers. %>
<%
  url = reader_safe_url(item.url)
  filter = local_assigns.fetch(:filter, nil)
  expanded = local_assigns.fetch(:expanded, false)
  chips = reader_chips(item, current_slug: params[:hobby])
  blurb = item.blurb(320)
%>
<article id="<%= dom_id(item) %>"
         class="reader-row group <%= "is-read" if item.read? %> <%= "is-open" if expanded %>"
         data-controller="reader-row"
         data-reader-row-read-url-value="<%= read_feed_item_path(item, from: "expand") %>"
         data-reader-row-dismiss-url-value="<%= read_feed_item_path(item, from: filter) %>"
         data-reader-row-read-value="<%= item.read? %>"
         data-river-target="row" data-ledger-target="row"
         data-ledger-id-param="<%= item.id %>">

  <%# The collapsed line. One fixed-height row at every width: face, the
      headline over one small signal line, the age. %>
  <div class="reader-line" data-action="click->reader-row#toggle">
    <%= feed_face(item.feed) %>

    <%# On a wide screen the source gets its own fixed column, so four
        hundred headlines all start at the same x. Below `lg` it drops into
        the signal line under the headline, where it costs no height. %>
    <span class="reader-src"><%= reader_source(item.feed) %></span>

    <div class="min-w-0 flex-1 lg:truncate">
      <h3 class="reader-headline">
        <% if item.saved? %><span class="text-sage-700" title="Saved" aria-label="Saved">★</span><% end %>
        <% if url %>
          <%= link_to item.title, url, class: "reader-title", title: item.title,
                data: { action: "reader-row#openOrExpand" }, **ReaderHelper::OUTBOUND %>
        <% else %>
          <span class="reader-title"><%= item.title %></span>
        <% end %>
      </h3>
      <%# The opening words trail the headline on a wide screen and are cut
          rather than wrapped. On a phone they are not here at all: they live
          in the expanded state, so the preview is paid for on the one item
          being considered rather than on all forty. %>
      <% if blurb.present? %>
        <span class="reader-blurb"> — <%= blurb.truncate(200) %></span>
      <% end %>

      <%# What the wide screen puts in its own columns, folded into one small
          line on a phone. A chip only appears here when it discriminates
          (see reader_chips) — a label on every row is texture. %>
      <div class="reader-signal">
        <span class="truncate"><%= reader_source(item.feed) %></span>
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        <span class="tabular-nums"><%= reader_age(item.published_on) %></span>
        <% chips.each do |section| %>
          <span class="chip <%= section_chip_classes(section) %>"><%= section.name %></span>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%# Dimmed at rest rather than absent: at `opacity-0` a pointer user
        never learned these existed, and Task — the verb that turns reading
        into doing, which is the whole point of the section — was the one
        nobody discovered. %>
    <div class="reader-verbs">
      <%= render "reader/verbs", item: item, filter: filter %>
    </div>

    <time class="reader-age" datetime="<%= item.published_on.iso8601 %>"
          title="<%= l(item.published_on, format: :long) %>">
      <%= reader_age(item.published_on) %>
    </time>
  </div>

  <%# The expanded state: what you need to decide whether to read the thing,
      and the verbs at a size a thumb can actually hit. Rendered with the row
      rather than fetched, so opening is instant. %>
  <%# Exactly one child, because the collapse is `grid-template-rows: 0fr`
      and that sizes the *first* row only — a second child lands in an
      implicit auto row and hangs out of the closed panel. %>
  <div class="reader-expand" data-reader-row-target="panel">
    <div class="min-h-0">
      <p class="reader-expand-text">
        <%= blurb.presence || "This feed sends headlines only — no summary came with this one." %>
      </p>
      <div class="reader-expand-verbs">
        <% if url %>
          <%= link_to url, class: "btn btn-primary reader-expand-open", **ReaderHelper::OUTBOUND do %>
            Open<span aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
        <%= render "reader/verbs", item: item, filter: filter, size: :lg %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
